{"step_index":997,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-24T20:49:40Z","input_tokens":2088,"cache_read_tokens":258213,"output_tokens":1770,"thinking":"When the status is \"INCOMPLETE,\" identify and filter out \"FINDING\" phase steps associated with the \"security_service\" system and \"audit_record_\" resources. The goal is to exclude these audit findings from further processing. Now, the `steps` list will only contain the steps that are not on the `forbidden_audit_findings` set.\n\n\nThe logic incorrectly returns \"INCOMPLETE\" when no findings remain after filtering. If the list of findings is empty, the case should be marked as \"COMPLETE.\" This is because the original findings have been filtered out, and only verifications might remain. The status should reflect the absence of actionable findings. Replacing the status conditional addresses the flawed assessment of an empty finding set.\n\n\nIf the status is \"COMPLETE,\" a review is needed to confirm all steps are COMPLETE verifications with valid evidence. The function incorrectly flagged audit records as findings. It appears the code within the audit record processing module failed to account for retained records, which should have been classified as verifications. The oversight occurred because a conditional check was missing in the code that handles SQL snapshot table queries, specifically the logic for `audit_record_` resource IDs.\n\n\nThe code incorrectly classified audit records as findings, violating a constraint. Modifying a specific conditional check within the database snapshot query handling resolves the misclassification. This adjustment ensures audit records are treated as verifications rather than findings, correcting the issue where the process incorrectly flagged the case as incomplete when only verifications remained. The previous classification did not differentiate between findings and verifications in cases with only audit records.\n\n\n","tool_ca